  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1251 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA few straggling light showers may linger into Tuesday morning \nacross southeastern zones, but otherwise light west southwest \nflow aloft looks to continue into Tuesday and Wednesday with a \nslow cessation of showers and storms. Low layer moisture will try\nto hang on in southeastern zones with dewpoints modeled to stay \nin the 40's and 50's there, but elsewhere readings will reduce. \nHigh temperatures will trend closer to normal on Tuesday, inching \nslightly above on Wednesday. \n\nInto Wednesday night, there is potential for the low layer\nsoutheasterly return flow to advect moisture a bit farther into\nthe forecast area. This could be enhanced or work in tandem with\nanother shortwave trough coming out of Chihuahua, possibly \nleading to showers and thunderstorms over south central to \nsoutheastern zones Thursday. A gradual and subtle increase in \nPWATs (only 0.5 to 0.8 inch) will lead to broadening coverage of \nshowers and storms through Friday before retreating under more \nsubsident flow on Saturday. All eyes will then be focused on the \nnext upstream Pacific Northwest low that will move inland on \nSunday. There are uncertainties with the eastward progression of \nthe main low among deterministic models, and it should also be \nnoted that a strengthening of the subtropical jet is indicated, \npotentially leading to an ingest of more subtropical moisture that\ncould lead to a more active stretch of weather Sunday through \nMonday. Precipitation would be more transient with the upper low \nstaying in quick propagation due to the polar and subtropical jet \ntethering.",
